<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarvastivada</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarvastivada was one of the early schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, named after its doctrine that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharmas</a>, the elements of existence, exist in past, present and future. Strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from about the 2nd century BCE, it produced a large Abhidharma literature, including the Mahavibhasa, and its monastic code was followed in oasis states such as Kucha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karasahr</a>. Its ideas shaped later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarvastivada.jpg" alt="Sarvastivada" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marek Gawęcki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tapa_Shotor_seated_Buddha_(Niche_V1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sarvastivada was one of the early Buddhist schools, established in the time of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. Its name is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> term glossed as 'the theory of all that exists'. According to K. L. Dhammajoti, the school and its rival, the Vibhajyavada lineage, were both present in Ashoka's reign, so that it was already a distinct school.</p><p>According to some accounts, the Sarvastivadins emerged from the Sthavira Nikaya, a small group of conservatives, and were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, after which they moved to north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Charles Prebish observes that a great deal of mystery surrounds the school's rise and early development. Its beginnings have also been linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s sending of Majjhantika on a mission to Gandhara, and a community of Sarvastivadin monks was said to have been established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> by the patriarch Upagupta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The central doctrine held that all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharmas</a>, the elementary components of experience, exist in the past, present and future. It was justified partly from the Buddha's teaching on karma: since good or bad actions ripen into fruit, the dharmas involved must still exist when the fruit appears, even though the cause lies in the past.</p><p>The school also taught momentariness, causal simultaneity, conditionality and its own account of the spiritual path. The Vaibhasikas, the orthodox Kashmiri branch, held that only present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharmas</a> have efficacy, and that each dharma has a constant essence, its svabhava, through all three times.</p><p>The school had a distinct Abhidharma literature of seven canonical texts, among them the Jnanaprasthana, together with the Mahavibhasa, a great commentary compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> around the reign of the Kushan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a>. All were translated into Chinese. Not every Sarvastivadin accepted the new orthodoxy: masters of Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, and the Sautrantikas who relied on the sutras, disagreed with it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Sarvastivadins flourished in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> until the 7th century CE. When the pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visited Kucha in 630 CE he found about one hundred convents and more than five thousand disciples of the school. The Sautrantika Vasubandhu wrote the Abhidharmakosa, which became the standard Abhidharma text in Indo-Tibetan and East Asian Buddhism, and he later turned to the Yogacara school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a>. Saṃghabhadra answered him for the Vaibhasikas.</p><p>The Mulasarvastivada Vinaya, whose relation to the school is uncertain, remains the monastic rule of Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the Sarvastivada Vinaya was once the commonest, until an imperial edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhongzong-of-tang">Emperor Zhongzong of Tang</a> in the early 8th century required the Dharmaguptaka Vinaya for ordination.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarvastivada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ruled by its own kings, Karasahr lived from irrigated farming, fishing, salt and Silk Road trade, and its monasteries at Shikshin followed the <b>Sarvastivada</b> school.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-karasahr">Kingdom of Karasahr</a></li></ul></section>
